<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Mongol invasion of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1240">1240</a>–<a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-europe">Mongol invasion of Europe</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The first Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was a raid by a Mongol army under Baidar and Orda in 1240–1241, covering the flank of the main invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked Sandomierz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and on 9 April 1241 defeated the Silesian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>, where he was killed. They then withdrew south to join Batu in Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-mongol-invasion-of-poland.jpg" alt="First Mongol invasion of Poland" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown / (of the reproduction) Wroclaw University Library / T. Zoltowska-Huszcza (sorry for ignoring diacritics) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HedwigManuscriptLiegnitz_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
